<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Iverni</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of south-west Ireland named by Ptolemy, whose name is linked to the Érainn and to Ériu, the Irish name for Ireland itself.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Ptolemy's Geography</a> of about 150 places the Iverni in the south-west of Ireland, around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>, with a town called Ivernis and a river Iernus nearby. Their name is related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hibernia">Ivernia</a> or Hibernia, the classical name for the island, and to the Érainn, a population group prominent in the early genealogies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Classical sources record nothing of their rulers or customs. Early Irish tradition remembers the Érainn as an ancient people of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Munster</a>, ancestors of groups such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corcu-duibne">Corcu Duibne</a>, who were powerful before the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a>. The region has many ogham stones, the earliest Irish writing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the Érainn had been eclipsed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a>, who built the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-munster">Kingdom of Munster</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-of-cashel">Cashel</a>. Érainn descendants such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcu-loigde">Corcu Loigde</a> survived as subordinate kingdoms in west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/iverni.jpg" alt="Iverni" width="320" height="409"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ptolemy%27s_Ireland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 200 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Goidelic Celtic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Iuerni, Érainn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=iverni" aria-label="Iverni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4513 335 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Iverni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4833 591h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4833 591h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ireland. <a href="/atlas?year=150&amp;focus=iverni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

